E. Playfair Cipher
Definition: Playfair is a polygraphic substitution cipher that encrypts pairs of letters using a 5×5 key matrix.

Purpose: Encrypts two letters together instead of one letter at a time.

Important rules:

Create a 5×5 matrix using a keyword.

Combine I and J in one cell.

Divide plaintext into pairs.

If a pair contains repeated letters, insert a filler such as X.

Apply the same row, column or rectangle rule.

Playfair Cipher — The Cipher Museum
Three encryption rules:

Same row: Take the letter to the right, wrapping around.

Same column: Take the letter below, wrapping around.

Different rows and columns: Use the rectangle rule, keeping the same rows and exchanging columns.

Viva question: Why is Playfair stronger than Caesar?

A: Playfair encrypts pairs of letters, making simple single-letter frequency analysis less effective.

